Target systems
==============

The main development work of the Geneva library collection is currently done
on Ubuntu Linux. Geneva is expected to compile on most reasonably recent Linux
distributions, provided a new enough compiler is available (see "Prerequisites"
below). Linux is the only regularly tested platform at this time.

Geneva has also been built experimentally on macOS using the bundled Clang
compiler, and much older versions were known to compile under Windows with the
Microsoft Visual C++ compiler. Neither is covered by regular testing today, so
your mileage may vary. Broader, regularly tested platform support is planned for
the future.

The authors would very much appreciate your feedback through the Geneva GitHub
project. In case you encounter any bugs or issues please let us know through the
Geneva bugtracker: https://github.com/gemfony/geneva-suite/issues .

For further information, please also have a look at our user manual, available
from the Downloads section of our web page http://www.gemfony.eu .


Hardware
========

Geneva is a 64-bit code base and requires a 64-bit processor of one of the two
mainstream families:

 - x86-64 (any modern Intel- or AMD-compatible 64-bit CPU), or
 - AArch64 (64-bit ARM).

Both are first-class, regularly tested targets. Two hardware features are used
when present but are never required:

 - SIMD: the random-number engine has a vectorised bulk-refill path (AVX2 on
   x86-64, NEON on AArch64), detected automatically at compile time, with a
   scalar fallback otherwise.
 - A CUDA-capable NVIDIA GPU: enables the optional GPU consumer and an optional
   CUDA random-number backend. Both fall back to a CPU implementation when no
   GPU or CUDA toolkit is present.


Prerequisites
=============

 - CMake, version 3.27 or later (the build system).

 - A C++23 compiler: the GNU Compiler Collection (g++) version 14 or later, or
   Clang version 18 or later.

 - The Boost libraries (see http://www.boost.org), version 1.91 or newer. Boost
   should be compiled from source and installed for the system, in
   C++23 mode (see "Compiling Boost on Linux" below). The
   distribution's Boost packages are frequently older than required and/or not
   built in C++23 mode, and are therefore not recommended.

 - Catch2, version 3, but only when building the test code.

 - The Git version control tool, only if you build from the version-control
   tree rather than from a released tarball.

Optional:

 - A CUDA toolkit, for the GPU consumer and/or the CUDA random-number backend.
 - An MPI implementation, for the MPI consumer.
 - A plotting viewer -- ROOT (http://root.cern), gnuplot, matplotlib or octave --
   to render the plot scripts that some examples emit. None of these is required to
   run Geneva or to produce the scripts, only to view them.


Installation prerequisites on Debian/Ubuntu
-------------------------------------------

The compiler and tools come from the distribution; Boost is compiled separately
(see "Compiling Boost on Linux" below):

sudo apt-get install --no-install-recommends cmake git g++ clang catch2


Building and installing Geneva
==============================

Geneva REQUIRES an out-of-source build: configure and compile in a directory
that is separate from the source checkout. The convenience script enforces this
and refuses to run inside the source tree. Use one of the two procedures below.


a) Using prepareBuild.sh and a configuration file
-------------------------------------------------

Geneva comes with a script that wraps the somewhat involved cmake call behind a
configuration file, so the chosen options can be recorded once and reused.
Assuming Geneva is in $HOME/geneva, create a build directory, copy the template
config over and adapt it:

mkdir -p $HOME/build && cd $HOME/build
cp $HOME/geneva/scripts/genevaConfig.gcfg ./myGenevaConfig.gcfg
# ... edit myGenevaConfig.gcfg (compiler, BOOSTROOT, build mode, install dir,
#     and the optional MPI/GPU/CUDA features) ...

Then configure (and optionally build) by handing the file to the script:

$HOME/geneva/scripts/prepareBuild.sh ./myGenevaConfig.gcfg --clean --build
sudo make install   # if installing into a system directory

Useful script options:

  --clean             return the build directory to a pristine state before
                      configuring
  --build             compile immediately after configuring, using all cores
                      (make -j$(nproc))
  --dryrun            print the exact cmake command that would be run, without
                      running it (handy for inspection or for an IDE)
  --generate-preset   write a CMakeUserPresets.json encoding the .gcfg settings,
                      for CLion or VS Code to pick up
  -y, --yes           assume "yes" for the interactive prompts
  --help, -h          show full usage


b) Direct call to cmake
-----------------------

Everything the script does reduces to a cmake invocation, which can be issued by
hand. From the build directory, point cmake at the source tree:

cd $HOME/build
cmake $HOME/geneva -DGENEVA_BUILD_TYPE="Release"
make -j$(nproc)
sudo make install

To choose a custom installation directory (the default is /opt/geneva):

cmake $HOME/geneva -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX="/where/the/library/goes"

The configuration variables map onto the .gcfg settings: INSTALLDIR becomes
CMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX, BUILDMODE becomes GENEVA_BUILD_TYPE (Release, Debug,
RelWithDebInfo, MinSizeRel or Sanitize), BUILDTESTCODE becomes
GENEVA_BUILD_TESTS, and so on; the compiler is selected with CMAKE_C_COMPILER /
CMAKE_CXX_COMPILER. The simplest way to see the full command for a given
configuration is to run "prepareBuild.sh ./my.gcfg --dryrun".

If Boost was installed in a non-standard location (for instance /opt/boost),
make it known to cmake either by its prefix:

cmake $HOME/geneva -DBOOST_ROOT="/opt/boost"

or, if headers and libraries do not share a prefix, by both locations:

cmake $HOME/geneva -DBOOST_INCLUDEDIR="/opt/boost/include" \
                   -DBOOST_LIBRARYDIR="/opt/boost/lib"

Adding -DCMAKE_VERBOSE_MAKEFILE="1" shows the compiler command lines.


Running the test suite
======================

A build with the test code enabled (BUILDTESTCODE in the .gcfg,
GENEVA_BUILD_TESTS in cmake -- both default to on) registers the entire suite
with CTest. Run it from the build directory:

cd $HOME/build
ctest

The suite runs in parallel; give CTest as many jobs as you have cores:

ctest -j$(nproc)

Every test is always registered; none is hidden behind a switch. What decides
whether a given run includes a test is its SIZE -- one label per test, chosen
from its measured runtime, so the choice is made when the tests are run rather
than when the tree is configured:

  S    under 1 second
  M    1 to 10 seconds
  L    10 to 60 seconds
  XL   over a minute

Every registered test carries exactly one of these. The three commands worth
remembering:

ctest -j$(nproc) -LE "L|XL"     while changing code -- seconds
ctest -j$(nproc) -LE XL         before a commit, and before a push
ctest -j$(nproc) -L XL          once per larger piece of work, and not before
                                a push -- it is a rot check, not a gate

Tests also carry descriptive labels, which are informational only and never
decide whether a test runs: the owning library (common, hap, weft, dietrich,
courtier, geneva), "example" for the end-to-end runs of projects/*/examples/,
and "benchmark" for the three programs whose whole purpose is a
convergence- or throughput-scale measurement (GOptimizationBenchmark,
GParallelisationOverhead and, in a GPU-consumer build, GCUDAOptBenchmark).
Those three happen to be the XL set today, but it is their size that keeps them
out of a commit gate, not their name -- a benchmark turned down to ten seconds
would be an L and would gate like any other L.

Adding a test? Measure it and give it a size. Run it once on an otherwise idle
machine with "ctest -j1 -R <name>" -- serial, because under -j a test's reported
time includes whatever it was competing with -- and pass the letter to the
registration:

  GENEVA_TEST_SIZE(<test-name> <S|M|L|XL>)          for a plain ADD_TEST
  geneva_<library>_test(<name> <size> <sources>)    for the per-library helpers

A test within about 20 % of a boundary is rounded UP, so a borderline case
lands in the more expensive class rather than sneaking into a fast loop. A size
that turns out to be wrong is an ordinary review fix: correct the letter, do not
work around it. Sizes are re-measured when a test's cost genuinely changes.

A handful of tests are registered only when what they need was actually found at
configure time -- the two MPI examples need an MPI launcher, example 11 needs a
Python 3 interpreter, and the GPU examples and the GPU benchmark exist only in a
build with the GPU consumer enabled. Configure says so for each one it leaves
out, so a missing test always has a stated reason.


Making the libraries known to the system on Linux
=================================================

After "make install" you have to make the installation directory known to the
dynamic linker, so that programs find the libraries at run time. System-wide,
create a file /etc/ld.so.conf.d/geneva.conf containing the library directory,
for example:

/opt/geneva/lib

then run "sudo ldconfig". Alternatively, set the location per session:

export LD_LIBRARY_PATH="/opt/geneva/lib:$LD_LIBRARY_PATH"

Follow the same procedure for Boost if you compiled your own version of it.


Consuming Geneva from your own project
======================================

An installed Geneva is best consumed through its CMake package:

  find_package(Geneva CONFIG REQUIRED)
  target_link_libraries(myTarget PRIVATE Geneva::geneva)

The imported targets are Geneva::common, Geneva::hap, Geneva::weft,
Geneva::dietrich, Geneva::courtier and Geneva::geneva; linking Geneva::geneva
pulls in the layers beneath it, the C++23
requirement and the Boost dependency transitively.

projects/geneva/quickstart/ is a working project of exactly this shape and the
recommended starting point: it is not built by the in-tree build, it locates
Geneva only through the installed package, and it can be copied out of the tree
as-is. Its README has the step-by-step standalone build.


Recommended software
====================

A Geneva optimization can emit ready-to-run plotting scripts for several backends
-- ROOT (see http://root.cern, a C++ analysis framework widely used in particle
physics), gnuplot, matplotlib or octave. Installing the viewer for whichever
backend you prefer lets you render the figures the examples emit. None of these is
required to run Geneva or to produce the scripts, only to view the results.


Compiling Boost on Linux
========================

Download a Boost release, unpack it, and configure the build with bootstrap.sh,
naming the installation prefix (here /opt/boost):

wget https://archives.boost.io/release/1.91.0/source/boost_1_91_0.tar.bz2
tar xjf boost_1_91_0.tar.bz2
cd boost_1_91_0
./bootstrap.sh --prefix=/opt/boost

Then compile and install with b2. The decisive flag is cxxflags="-std=c++23":
Boost must be built in the same C++23 mode as Geneva. Installing into /opt
requires root:

sudo ./b2 -j$(nproc) \
   cxxflags="-std=c++23" \
   variant=release \
   link=shared \
   threading=multi \
   install

To build only the components Geneva uses, add:
   --with-atomic --with-json --with-program_options --with-regex
To match Geneva's compiler too, pass "--with-toolset=clang" to bootstrap.sh and
"toolset=clang" to b2.

Afterwards, point genevaConfig.gcfg (BOOSTROOT="/opt/boost") or the cmake call
(-DBOOST_ROOT="/opt/boost") at this location, and do not forget to add an entry
for Boost to ldconfig, as discussed above.
